        "metadata": {
          "title": "Photon Quantization from Geometric Invariants in Spatial-Causal Geometry (SCG)",
          "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.16730165",
          "publication_date": "2025-08-02",
          "description": "<p>This paper presents a first-principles derivation of photon quantization using spatial-curvature invariants within the Spatial-Causal Geometry (SCG) framework. The photon is modeled not as a point particle or wavefunction excitation, but as a finite, transverse field structure constrained by causal curvature and the invariant ratio $\\gamma_{\\text{cause}} = r_\\gamma / \\lambda$. Quantized energy arises naturally from geometric boundary conditions, with Planck&rsquo;s constant emerging as a derived consequence of field coherence&mdash;not an imposed axiom.</p>\n<p>The SCG model eliminates the need for gauge symmetry and rejects the attribution of spin-1 to individual photons. All observed angular momentum phenomena are explained as ensemble-level Maxwell tension reorientations, not intrinsic rotation. Classical polarization effects&mdash;including linear, circular, and elliptical states&mdash;are rederived as field projection geometries rather than probabilistic state collapses.</p>\n<p>Malus&rsquo;s Law is shown to hold deterministically at the single-photon level, directly contradicting the binary premises underlying Bell&rsquo;s inequality. Beth&rsquo;s torque measurements are resolved as continuous field alignment effects, not evidence of photon spin. Experimental observations traditionally cited as proof of quantum nonlocality or spin angular momentum are re-explained as coherent, causal interactions governed by curvature dynamics.</p>\n<p>This geometric model offers a unified interpretation of photon structure, energy quantization, and polarization without resorting to wavefunction formalism, probabilistic collapse, or internal degrees of freedom. It redefines the photon as a causal topological entity&mdash;real, finite, and rotationless&mdash;providing a new foundation for both classical and quantum optics.</p>\n<p>Keywords<br>Spatial-Causal Geometry (SCG); photon quantization; polarization geometry; $\\gamma_{\\text{cause}}$; causal curvature; Maxwell tension; Beth experiment; Malus&rsquo;s Law; spin-1 photon rejection; gauge-free electrodynamics; structured photon; angular momentum reinterpretation; geometric optics; field coherence; nonlocality refutation; topological quantization</p>",
          "access_right": "open",
